 <p>Use examples such as <code>caco log perf-list --project cacophony --metric daemon_memory_rss_bytes --limit 20</code> or <code>caco log perf-list --project cacophony --top</code> to generate operator reports from the canonical telemetry store. Set <code>CACO_DAEMON_PERF_TELEMETRY_INTERVAL_SECS=0</code> only for a deliberate diagnostic opt-out; otherwise the default bounded cadence keeps telemetry available without writing on every request.</p>
 
+<h2>iOS/Android app error reports (platform-tagged)</h2>
+<p>Companion-app runtime errors &mdash; failed button actions, failed daemon requests, response decode failures &mdash; flow into Cacophony as first-class platform errors through the same structured-error ingest endpoint the CLI uses, so iOS/Android surface devs can analyse them in the Errors surfaces (<code>caco log exceptions</code>, the TUI/Web Errors tab).</p>
+<p><strong>Endpoint.</strong> <code>POST /api/v1/logs/error</code> &mdash; the same path <code>caco log error</code> uses, with fields <code>component</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>detail</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>fingerprint</code>, <code>labels</code>. The endpoint already exists (<code>handle_log_error</code>); clients do not need a new route.</p>
+<p><strong>Auth.</strong> The companion apps authenticate with their existing node/operator token. A node-identity caller is authorized for every endpoint (<code>CallerIdentity::Node</code> resolves to an allowed scope check), so the app's token can <code>POST /api/v1/logs/error</code> without a new scope grant; worker-scope tokens are not used by the apps.</p>
+<p><strong>Platform-tagging convention</strong> (stable &mdash; the iOS/Android client beads target this):</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>iOS</th><th>Android</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td><code>component</code></td><td><code>ios-app</code></td><td><code>android-app</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>label <code>platform:</code></td><td><code>platform:ios</code></td><td><code>platform:android</code></td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+<p>Recommended additional labels:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><code>surface:&lt;view&gt;</code> &mdash; the originating screen, e.g. <code>surface:ContentView</code>, <code>surface:AgentDetail</code>.</li>
+  <li><code>kind:&lt;class&gt;</code> &mdash; the failure class: <code>kind:button</code> (a failed UI action), <code>kind:request</code> (a failed daemon request), <code>kind:decode</code> (a response-decode failure).</li>
+</ul>
+<p><strong>Fingerprint guidance (dedup).</strong> Set <code>fingerprint</code> to a stable signature of the recurring failure, e.g. <code>&lt;component&gt;:&lt;surface&gt;:&lt;kind&gt;:&lt;stable-error-key&gt;</code>, omitting volatile detail (timestamps, request ids), so a repeatedly-failing button aggregates into one Errors row with an occurrence count instead of flooding the surface. When <code>fingerprint</code> is omitted the daemon derives one from the component and summary.</p>
+<p><strong>Filtering.</strong> Narrow the Errors surface to one platform with the existing component filter &mdash; <code>caco log exceptions --component ios-app</code> / <code>--component android-app</code> &mdash; plus the <code>platform:</code> / <code>surface:</code> / <code>kind:</code> labels for finer analysis.</p>
+<p><strong>Example</strong> (verified end to end):</p>
+<pre><code>caco log error --project cacophony \
+  --component ios-app \
+  --summary "Save button failed" \
+  --detail "ContentView save -&gt; POST /api/v1/agents/&lt;id&gt;/nudge returned 500" \
+  --severity error \
+  --labels "platform:ios,surface:ContentView,kind:button"
+# surfaces in: caco log exceptions --component ios-app</code></pre>
+
 <h2>Operator takeaway</h2>
 <p>If you want to watch an agent live, use attach/terminal surfaces. If you want history, use the structured log views. Do not expect <code>session.log</code> to exist for new agents; future embedded terminal panes should use the broker contract rather than adding new unbounded capture files.</p>
